<h1>Likutay Halachos — Hilchos Tefillin, Halacha 5<br><span style="font-size:18px; color:#7a622e;">Part D: Sections 26–33</span></h1>

<!-- =============== Section 26 =============== -->
<h3>§ 26</h3>

<p>Therefore the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span> are the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">Ehyeh Havayah Ehyeh</span>, which have the numerical value of <span class="key-term">Chayim</span>. For <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ibur</span> <span class="bracket-def">[gestation]</span>, as explained in the <span class="key-term">Kavanos</span>. As explained in the words of our Rebbe in the Torah "<span class="transliteration">Kera es Yehoshua</span>" <span class="source">(LM I:6)</span>: <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> — "<span class="zohar-quote">Ana zamin l'meihevei</span>" — "I am preparing to become." Before <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span>, one has no <span class="key-term">havayah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[existence/being]</span> at all. Only when one begins to do <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> is he in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span> — "<span class="zohar-quote">Ana zamin l'meihevei</span>" — he begins to prepare himself to have being in the world.</p>

<p>One must do <span class="bold-concept">teshuvah upon teshuvah</span>. Even a Tzaddik who has already done complete <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> must do <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> each time regarding his previous <span class="key-term">hasagah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[perception]</span> — for each time he comes to a new <span class="key-term">hasagah</span>. Then he does <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span> — upon yesterday's <span class="key-term">hasagah</span>, for he had materialized the loftiness of Hashem's G-dliness. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> — the World to Come, which is entirely <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, entirely <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span>.</p>

<p>Thus the true Tzaddik is <span class="bold-concept">always in the aspect of Ehyeh</span> — for each time he comes to new <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>, beginning to perceive Hashem with new perception, which is the long life of living anew each time. Each day he is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span> — "<span class="zohar-quote">Ana zamin l'meihevei</span>" — as if he has not yet begun to live at all and has had no <span class="key-term">havayah</span> in the world. Only now does he begin to live and prepare himself to have being. This is <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — long life where the Tzaddik begins anew each time — are in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Ehyeh Havayah Ehyeh</span> = <span class="key-term">Chayim</span>. Each day: <span class="bold-concept">Ehyeh</span> (= <span class="key-term">ibur</span>, "<span class="zohar-quote">ana zamin l'meihevei</span>," beginning to live as if he had no <span class="key-term">havayah</span> or <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> at all) → then new complete <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> are drawn = <span class="bold-concept">Havayah</span> (= the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>) → immediately upon receiving these <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> (= life), he withdraws them; only the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> remains; then he yearns again to receive even greater new <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> = <span class="bold-concept">second Ehyeh</span> ("<span class="zohar-quote">ana zamin l'meihevei</span>"). And so forever. These three Names = <span class="key-term">Chayim</span> = the essential long life.</p>

<p>But the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span> — <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span> — are the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">Ehyeh Havayah Adnus</span>. They too must begin anew each day (= <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span>), then receive their <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> (= <span class="key-term">Havayah</span>). But when their <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> depart, they remain in the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">Adnus</span> — <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, <span class="key-term">emunah</span> — believing that Hashem is <span class="key-term">Adon Kol</span> <span class="bracket-def">[Master of all]</span>. This point of <span class="key-term">Adnus</span>/<span class="key-term">emunah</span> never departs — the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> must always remain. For the common people, the essential renewal of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> is so that they remain in strong <span class="key-term">emunah</span> — which is their essential <span class="key-term">chiyus</span>, the foundation of everything.</p>

<p>But the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span> — the Tzaddik's own <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> — remain in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span>: he merits the true long life of beginning anew each time. This is <span class="bold-concept">Ehyeh Asher Ehyeh</span> — <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> upon <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span>. Each time he begins to live anew in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span>. "<span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span>" = "<span class="zohar-quote">Ana zamin l'meihevei</span>" — beginning to live and receive new <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. "<span class="key-term">Asher</span>" = letters of "<span class="key-term">Rosh</span>" = the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> = <span class="key-term">Havayah</span>. Then he returns to <span class="key-term">Ehyeh</span> — yearning to receive even greater new <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> and <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. Thus <span class="bold-concept">Ehyeh Asher Ehyeh = Ehyeh Havayah Ehyeh = Tefillin</span>. This Name was revealed to Moshe at the time of <span class="key-term">Yetzias Mitzrayim</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<p>Likewise, each individual must draw upon himself these two aspects of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>: <span class="key-term">shel Yad</span> and <span class="key-term">shel Rosh</span>. When the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> depart, through the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> remaining in the heart, one draws <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> and <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> to strengthen himself in <span class="key-term">emunah</span> = <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span> (whose Names = <span class="key-term">Yabok</span> = <span class="key-term">baki</span> = passing over all obstacles). Then one is aroused with <span class="key-term">kinah</span> for himself and draws great <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> to rise from level to level, living new <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each time = <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span> (whose Names = <span class="key-term">Ehyeh Havayah Ehyeh</span> = <span class="key-term">Chayim</span>). Understand well.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 27 =============== -->
<h3>§ 27</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">Tefillin of Rashi</span> and <span class="bold-concept">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span>. It is explained in the <span class="key-term">Kavanos</span> that <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span> are far loftier <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>, where the <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> <span class="bracket-def">[judgments]</span> emerge before the <span class="key-term">Chassadim</span> <span class="bracket-def">[kindnesses]</span> — the order of <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span>: <span class="key-term">Yud-Kei-Kuf-Vav</span> (= <span class="transliteration">Kadeish, V'hayah ki yevi'acha, V'hayah im shamoa, Shema</span>). <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rashi</span> are <span class="key-term">Havayah</span> in proper order — <span class="key-term">Chassadim</span> before <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span>.</p>

<p>For at first it arose in Hashem's thought to create the world with <span class="key-term">Middas HaDin</span> <span class="bracket-def">[the Attribute of Justice]</span> alone. He saw the world could not endure and He placed <span class="key-term">Middas HaRachamim</span> <span class="bracket-def">[Mercy]</span> first, combining it with <span class="key-term">Din</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Eileh toldos hashamayim v'ha'aretz b'hibar'am b'yom asos Hashem Elokim eretz v'shamayim</span>" <span class="source">(Bereishis 2:4)</span>.</p>

<p>The <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> and the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> are drawn from the <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> and <span class="key-term">Dinim</span> originating in the <span class="bold-concept">initial <span class="key-term">Tzimtzum</span></span> of the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> <span class="bracket-def">[Empty Space]</span>. Breaking the <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> comes from <span class="key-term">Middas HaRachamim</span> that was combined with <span class="key-term">Din</span> — giving power to break the <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> and transform it to holiness. This is what the Tzaddikim do: transform <span class="key-term">Din</span> to <span class="key-term">Rachamim</span>.</p>

<p>At first, Hashem wanted man to overcome the <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> by his own power, without heavenly assistance — <span class="key-term">Din</span> alone. But He saw this was impossible, so He placed <span class="key-term">Rachamim</span> before <span class="key-term">Din</span>. From this comes Hashem's constant mercy — sending <span class="key-term">hirhurei teshuvah</span> and assisting man to break the <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span>. As our Sages said <span class="source">(Kiddushin 30b)</span>: "Each day a man's <span class="key-term">Yetzer</span> overpowers him — were it not that Hashem helps him, he would fall into its hands." The <span class="key-term">Yetzer</span> overpowering each day = the <span class="key-term">Din</span> of the initial thought being aroused each day. But Hashem immediately has mercy and places <span class="key-term">Rachamim</span> before <span class="key-term">Din</span> = the help and assistance.</p>

<p>However, there are <span class="bold-concept">great Tzaddikim who do not need heavenly assistance</span>. Hashem conducts Himself with them initially with <span class="key-term">Middas HaDin</span>, and they withstand all trials, enduring all manner of suffering and troubles drawn from <span class="key-term">Din</span>, and remain steadfast. As it says about Avraham and the Avos: "<span class="transliteration">HaElokim asher hishalchu avosai lefanav</span>" <span class="source">(Bereishis 48:15)</span> — "<span class="key-term">HaElokim</span>" precisely = <span class="key-term">Middas HaDin</span>, walking on his own without heavenly assistance, as Rashi explains in Parshas Noach.</p>

<p>This is the suffering of the Tzaddikim: "<span class="transliteration">Hashem Tzaddik yivchan</span>" — "Hashem tests the righteous" <span class="source">(Tehillim 11:5)</span> — like a flax worker: the better his flax, the harder he strikes it <span class="source">(Bereishis Rabbah 32)</span>. "<span class="transliteration">Ki es asher ye'ehav Hashem yochiach</span>" — "Whom Hashem loves, He rebukes" <span class="source">(Mishlei 3:12)</span>. They sweeten the <span class="key-term">Din</span> at its root in the initial thought. They believe and know that the suffering and <span class="key-term">Dinim</span> themselves are great goods. Through this they merit to ascend and be included in the initial thought.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">Rabbi Akiva</span>, whose flesh was combed with iron combs. Moshe Rabbeinu asked: "This is Torah and this is its reward?!" Hashem answered: "<span class="bold-concept">Silence! Thus it arose in the thought.</span>" The suffering of such Tzaddikim is drawn from the initial thought — creating through <span class="key-term">Din</span> alone — so they would merit to stand the trial without heavenly assistance and thereby be included in the initial thought.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 28 =============== -->
<h3>§ 28</h3>

<p>For the essential <span class="key-term">Din</span> is drawn from the initial <span class="key-term">Tzimtzum</span> of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span></span>. As explained in the Torah "<span class="transliteration">Bo el Paroh</span>" <span class="source">(LM I:64)</span>: the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">questions that cannot be answered</span>. But the great Tzaddik — the aspect of Moshe — can enter even these questions, the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>. This is the Tzaddik ascending to the initial thought — the beginning of the <span class="key-term">Tzimtzum</span>, the root of <span class="key-term">Din</span> — and withstanding the trial there, enduring all suffering and <span class="key-term">Dinim</span>. Through this he merits to perceive the secret of the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>: <span class="bold-concept">that even there, Hashem is concealed and hidden in a wondrous and awesome manner that cannot be grasped</span>, as written there.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span></span> — loftier <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> where <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> precede <span class="key-term">Chassadim</span> — drawn from these higher Tzaddikim. Hashem conducts Himself with them through the initial thought. They merit to be included in the initial thought and sweeten everything at its root. This is "<span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> before <span class="key-term">Chassadim</span>": they can receive the <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> themselves before <span class="key-term">Chassadim</span> — the conduct of the initial thought — for they merit even through <span class="key-term">Middas HaDin</span> itself, having endured great suffering and been tested.</p>

<p>But <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rashi</span></span> — <span class="key-term">Chassadim</span> before <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> — is the aspect of placing <span class="key-term">Rachamim</span> before <span class="key-term">Din</span>, which is the essential conduct of the entire world. Therefore the main <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are those of Rashi, which most of the world wears — for the world's essential conduct and endurance is only through this. But one who wishes to sanctify himself with additional holiness should also wear <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span> — to receive illumination from those higher Tzaddikim who merited to ascend to the initial thought and sweeten all <span class="key-term">Dinim</span> at their very root.</p>

<p>Therefore <span class="bold-concept">our Rebbe instructed his followers to wear <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span></span>. For all distancing from Hashem comes from the <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> whose root is in <span class="key-term">Middas HaDin</span> drawn from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>. The essential additional holiness is drawn through <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span> — from the Tzaddikim who ascend to the root of <span class="key-term">Din</span> in the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> and sweeten everything at its root. Through this, power is drawn for all who are far away to break and nullify the <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> drawn from there.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 29 =============== -->
<h3>§ 29</h3>

<p>Therefore now, in the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Ikvesa d'Meshicha</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[the heels of Mashiach's footsteps]</span>, at the end of the <span class="key-term">galus</span>, one must be very careful to wear <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span>, as all the true Tzaddikim of our days urged. For now the <span class="key-term">Baal Davar</span> has strengthened exceedingly — seeing that his end is near, he exerts all his power, like two men wrestling: when one sees he is about to fall, he rallies with all his strength to topple the other. As the wise man said: <span class="bold-concept">"There is no warrior like the desperate one."</span></p>

<p>The <span class="key-term">galus</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ibur</span> <span class="bracket-def">[gestation]</span>; leaving the <span class="key-term">galus</span> = <span class="key-term">leidah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[birth]</span>. As with <span class="key-term">Geulas Mitzrayim</span>, which is called birth: "<span class="transliteration">Umolodosayich b'yom huleches osach</span>" <span class="source">(Yechezkel 16:4)</span>. And regarding the final <span class="key-term">Geulah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Ki chalah gam yaldah Tziyon es baneha</span>" — "For Tziyon has travailed and also given birth to her children" <span class="source">(Yeshayah 66:8)</span>. Therefore at the end of the <span class="key-term">galus</span>, the <span class="key-term">galus</span> intensifies — as in Mitzrayim: "<span class="transliteration">Umei'az basi el Paroh l'daber bismecha heira la'am hazeh</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">Tichbad ha'avodah</span>" <span class="source">(Shemos 5:22-23)</span>. This is <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[difficult labor]</span> — "<span class="transliteration">Vayhi ki hikshah Paroh l'shalcheinu</span>" <span class="source">(Shemos 13:15)</span>.</p>

<p>All the suffering endured now at the end of this final <span class="key-term">galus</span> are the aspect of <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">chevlei leidah</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[birth pangs]</span>: "<span class="transliteration">K'mo harah sakriv laledes tachil tiz'ak bachavaleha</span>." The essential intensification of the <span class="key-term">galus</span> is that the <span class="key-term">Baal Davar</span> strengthens mightily over the souls of Yisrael to distance them from Hashem. This is the essential <span class="key-term">galus</span> — the suffering of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>. Beyond this, everything is <span class="key-term">hevel</span>. The essential trouble is that he introduces <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">kefiros</span> and <span class="key-term">apikorsus</span></span> into the world. As our Rebbe said: "A great <span class="key-term">apikorsus</span> is going through the world." As we see with our own eyes — never was there such <span class="key-term">apikorsus</span> in Yisrael as now, when the brazen ones among the nation teach the youth their wisdoms and their languages.</p>

<p>All this intensification of the <span class="key-term">Baal Davar</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Chevlei Mashiach</span> = <span class="key-term">chevlei leidah</span>. All his power derives from the root of <span class="key-term">Din</span> in the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>. Therefore now the only power to stand against him is through the great true Tzaddikim who can enter the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> and sweeten the <span class="key-term">Din</span> at its root — nullifying all the power of the <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> of all the world's inhabitants drawn from there. Therefore each person must be careful to wear <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span> — drawn from the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of these great Tzaddikim — to receive power from them to break the <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> that is strengthening so greatly now.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 30 =============== -->
<h3>§ 30</h3>

<p>For the essential <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span> derives from <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Amalek</span></span>, as understood from our Rebbe's words <span class="source">(LM I:135)</span> on the verse "<span class="transliteration">Vay'shasef Shmuel es Agag</span>" — how Shmuel cut Agag into four, through which the womb of the woman with <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span> was opened. <span class="key-term">Amalek</span> = the <span class="key-term">kefiros</span> and <span class="key-term">apikorsus</span> drawn from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>. Therefore he is called "<span class="key-term">Reishis</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">Reishis goyim Amalek</span>" <span class="source">(Bamidbar 24:20)</span> — for the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> preceded the world (without it there would be no place for creation). From there comes the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">kelipah</span> that precedes the fruit</span>. Therefore <span class="key-term">Amalek</span> is called <span class="key-term">Reishis</span> — his power from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> that preceded the world.</p>

<p>This is the <span class="bold-concept">firstborn right of Eisav</span>, who is <span class="key-term">Amalek</span> — from the <span class="key-term">kelipos</span> drawn from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> that preceded the world. Therefore Yaakov strove to take the <span class="key-term">bechorah</span> from Eisav — for in truth <span class="bold-concept">Hashem preceded everything</span>: He fills all worlds and surrounds all worlds, and He Himself created the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> for the sake of creation. Through their <span class="key-term">emunah</span> in Hashem who preceded everything, <span class="bold-concept">Yisrael pass over all the wisdoms and <span class="key-term">kefiros</span> and <span class="key-term">apikorsus</span></span> that come from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> — believing that even within the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> itself, Hashem is concealed. He preceded everything. Therefore Yisrael are called <span class="key-term">Ivri'im</span> — through their faith in the G-d of the <span class="key-term">Ivri'im</span>, they <span class="bold-concept">pass over</span> all the wisdoms that come from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>. Therefore Yisrael are truly the firstborn: "<span class="transliteration">B'ni bechori Yisrael</span>" <span class="source">(Shemos 4:22)</span>.</p>

<p>Yaakov took the <span class="key-term">bechorah</span> from Eisav. Initially the <span class="key-term">bechorah</span> had to be with Eisav — for the <span class="key-term">kelipah</span> precedes the fruit, and his firstborn right derived from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> that preceded the world. But Yaakov, through his <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, merited to pass over the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> and believe and be included in Hashem who preceded everything. Afterward he nullified Eisav's <span class="key-term">bechorah</span> (= <span class="key-term">Reishis goyim Amalek</span>) and took it for himself, until his offspring were all called <span class="key-term">bechorim</span>: "<span class="transliteration">B'ni bechori Yisrael.</span>"</p>

<p>This is the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Makkas Bechoros</span></span> at the time of <span class="key-term">Yetzias Mitzrayim</span>, when the firstborn of Yisrael were sanctified. For all the exiles — included in <span class="key-term">Galus Mitzrayim</span> — derive from the contamination of <span class="key-term">Amalek</span> (= the essential contamination of the serpent = <span class="key-term">Reishis goyim</span>), from whom all four kingdoms receive, as our Rebbe said in the Torah "<span class="transliteration">Mishra d'Sakina</span>" <span class="source">(LM I:30)</span>. The prince of Mitzrayim is Edom = Eisav. Paroh's hardness and heaviness of heart — refusing to let Yisrael leave — came from the <span class="key-term">apikorsus</span> and <span class="key-term">kefiros</span> drawn from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> = <span class="key-term">Amalek</span>. Therefore Hashem killed all the firstborn of Mitzrayim — to subdue the <span class="key-term">bechor</span> of the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> drawn from Eisav's firstborn right (= the <span class="key-term">kefiros</span> from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> that preceded the world) — and to elevate the firstborn of Yisrael, who receive from Yaakov's <span class="key-term">bechorah</span> based on <span class="key-term">emunah</span> that passes over the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>.</p>

<p>Through this, Yisrael left Mitzrayim (= birth). Then the firstborn were sanctified to be given to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> — who receives his holiness from the holy elder, the aspect of <span class="key-term">z'kan Aharon</span> — <span class="key-term">Tikkunei D'ikna</span> drawn through the Blind Beggar who was included in the <span class="key-term">Ein Sof</span> that preceded everything (where the whole world does not amount to the blink of an eye). This sanctifies the firstborn — first birth — drawing the holiness of <span class="key-term">bechorah</span> of Yisrael, which is <span class="key-term">emunah</span>. "<span class="transliteration">V'ha'avarta kol peter rechem LaHashem</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">V'ha'avarta</span>" precisely — through the holiness of the firstborn, one passes over the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> via <span class="key-term">emunah</span>.</p>

<p>Therefore the <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> speak of <span class="key-term">Yetzias Mitzrayim</span>, the holiness of the firstborn, and <span class="key-term">emunah</span> — for they are all one. Through <span class="key-term">emunah</span> one passes over all the wisdoms from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>. This = <span class="key-term">Yetzias Mitzrayim</span> = holiness of the firstborn. From there come the essential <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — the renewal of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span>. The essential renewal of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> is received through <span class="key-term">emunah</span> — the essential life: "<span class="transliteration">B'or pnei melech chayim</span>" <span class="source">(Mishlei 16:15)</span>. Through <span class="key-term">emunah</span> in <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Chidush HaOlam</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[the Creation of the world]</span> — that Hashem created everything as He willed, <span class="key-term">yesh mei'ayin hamuchlat</span> <span class="bracket-def">[something from absolute nothing]</span> — just as He renewed the world from absolute nothingness, so too He renews now in His goodness each day, constantly, <span class="key-term">Ma'aseh Bereishis</span>. The essential daily renewal of <span class="key-term">Ma'aseh Bereishis</span> is through <span class="key-term">Chochmah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Kulam b'Chochmah asisa</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 104:24)</span> — the new <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> drawn each day to recognize His light anew. "<span class="transliteration">Uvtuvo mechadesh b'chol yom tamid ma'aseh Bereishis</span>" — "<span class="key-term">Ain tov ela Torah, ain tov ela Tzaddik</span>" — the true Tzaddik merits new Torah perception and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> each day, through which <span class="key-term">Ma'aseh Bereishis</span> is renewed. From there: the essential <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<p>For truly it is all one: the birth of a Jewish <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> and the nullification of <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span> in the physical sense is literally the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yetzias Mitzrayim</span> (= birth), after Hashem nullified <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span> through <span class="key-term">Makkas Bechoros</span>. For at <span class="key-term">Yetzias Mitzrayim</span> (= birth), the holy <span class="key-term">emunah</span> was revealed — <span class="key-term">emunas Chidush HaOlam</span> — through the awesome signs and wonders Hashem performed.</p>

<p>Each new Jewish <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> that is born is a <span class="bold-concept">new revelation and new <span class="key-term">Da'as</span></span> to recognize the One who spoke and the world came into being. For Hashem never does the same thing twice — as our Rebbe said: even reincarnated <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> do not return identically, for Hashem never repeats. Each birth = a new <span class="key-term">seichel</span> to know Hashem with new knowledge. In each generation His G-dliness is revealed with new recognition. For this Hashem "troubles Himself" from generation to generation — to purify the <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> more and more from the serpent's contamination. "<span class="transliteration">Zeh Shmi l'olam v'zeh zichri l'dor dor</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">L'dor vador yeshabach ma'asecha.</span>"</p>

<p>Therefore each time a new <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> comes — new <span class="key-term">seichel</span> and new knowledge to recognize Him, to reveal His <span class="key-term">emunah</span> further — the contamination of <span class="key-term">Amalek</span> (the serpent's contamination) is aroused and seeks to obstruct the birth. This is <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span> — through the serpent's contamination (Adam's sin: "<span class="transliteration">B'etzev teildi vanim</span>"). Our Rebbe said that <span class="key-term">Mizmor l'Sodah</span> is an effective remedy for a woman in difficult labor — because it speaks of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">De'u ki Hashem Hu Elokim</span>," ending: "<span class="transliteration">V'ad dor vador emunaso.</span>" The essential <span class="key-term">emunah</span> is renewed and strengthened from generation to generation through the renewal of <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> (= new intelligences recognizing Hashem). Therefore through <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span> is nullified. The main remedy is <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> = <span class="key-term">emunah</span>.</p>

<p>"<span class="transliteration">Milchamah LaHashem ba'Amalek midor dor</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">midor dor</span>" precisely — he intensifies in each generation. He is an <span class="bold-concept">old and foolish king</span>, not wanting the renewal of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> that comes through the renewal of <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> each generation. He denies <span class="key-term">Chidush HaOlam</span>. Therefore the <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> begin with birth — "<span class="transliteration">Kadeish li chol bechor</span>" — for birth = the renewal of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>: to renew one's brain and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each day, beginning <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span> anew each day.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 32 =============== -->
<h3>§ 32</h3>

<p>Therefore now, at the end of the <span class="key-term">galus</span> — when the <span class="key-term">Baal Davar</span> intensifies exceedingly (= the contamination of the serpent, <span class="key-term">Haman-Amalek</span>, <span class="key-term">Chevlei Mashiach</span> = <span class="key-term">chevlei leidah</span> = <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span>) — the birth pangs and difficult labor are now far heavier than at <span class="key-term">Geulas Mitzrayim</span>, because now the final <span class="key-term">Geulah</span> will come — a <span class="bold-concept">complete <span class="key-term">Geulah</span> with no <span class="key-term">galus</span> after it</span>. Then the serpent's contamination will be completely nullified, all Yisrael will return to Hashem in truth forever, until we merit <span class="key-term">Techiyas HaMeisim</span>, when the world will be renewed: "<span class="transliteration">Bila hamaves lanetzach</span>" — "Death will be swallowed up forever" <span class="source">(Yeshayah 25:8)</span>. Then we will live long, eternal life = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = <span class="key-term">Eitz HaChayim</span>.</p>

<p>Because the <span class="key-term">Baal Davar</span> sees his end approaching, he intensifies exceedingly. His essential strategy is to make Yisrael fall into <span class="bold-concept">spiritual <span class="key-term">ziknah</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[old age / exhaustion]</span>: as if we have already grown old in this long <span class="key-term">galus</span> of so many hundreds of years, and there is no more hope. He causes each individual to fall into this <span class="key-term">ziknah</span>: as if one has already aged in his deeds and can no longer return. But the essential <span class="key-term">Geulah</span> depends on <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span>, as the Zohar HaKadosh says: "This matter depends on nothing but <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span>."</p>

<p>We pray: "<span class="transliteration">Al tashlicheinu l'eis ziknah, kichlos kocheinu al ta'azveinu</span>" — "Cast us not away in the time of old age; when our strength fails, forsake us not" <span class="source">(Tehillim 71:9)</span> — that we should not fall into the <span class="key-term">ziknah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span>. The <span class="key-term">Baal Davar</span> (<span class="key-term">Haman-Amalek</span>) is called an <span class="bold-concept">old and foolish king</span> — denying <span class="key-term">Chidush HaOlam</span>. From this denial comes the <span class="key-term">ziknah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> — as if the physical world is old and aged from long ago. The serpent said to Chavah: "<span class="transliteration">Min ha'eitz achal uvara es ha'olam</span>" — "From the Tree He ate and created the world" <span class="source">(Rashi, Bereishis 3:5)</span> — denying <span class="key-term">Chidush HaOlam</span>. Through this, death was decreed — the opposite of long life.</p>

<p>The elders who lack completeness — who do not add holiness and <span class="key-term">da'as</span> each day — give strength to these <span class="key-term">kefiros</span> (= the <span class="key-term">metzach hanachash</span> <span class="bracket-def">[forehead of the serpent]</span>, as in LM II:4). Since they do not add holiness and give foothold to the <span class="key-term">ziknah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span>, the <span class="key-term">kefiros</span> of the serpent's forehead — denying <span class="key-term">Chidush HaOlam</span> — are strengthened. All these <span class="key-term">kefiros</span>, from which the essential <span class="key-term">Yetzer HaRa</span> intensifies now at the end of the <span class="key-term">galus</span>, derive from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> — from which come all the questions of the philosophers that cannot be answered, all the <span class="key-term">apikorsus</span> and <span class="key-term">kefiros</span>, and the root of all evil desires. For the root of all evil desire is <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">eil acher</span> — <span class="key-term">kefiros</span></span>, as our Rebbe explained.</p>

<p>Therefore one must wear <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span> — the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> drawn from the Tzaddikim who can enter the initial <span class="key-term">Tzimtzum</span> of the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>, sweeten the <span class="key-term">Din</span> there, and reveal Hashem's G-dliness — informing humanity that even the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> and all the questions and <span class="key-term">kefiros</span> themselves derive from Hashem, who sustains everything. <span class="bold-concept">It is impossible to know Him except through the absence of knowledge that preceded knowledge</span> — the questions of the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>, the withdrawal of <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> (His light that was withdrawn from the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span>) — in order to create a place for the world, so that His G-dliness could be revealed in the world, as fully explained in "<span class="transliteration">Bo el Paroh</span>."</p>

<p>Therefore one must place <span class="key-term">emunah</span> before the world — this is the essence. Through this, one breaks the <span class="key-term">kelipah</span> that precedes the fruit (= the <span class="key-term">Chalal HaPanui</span> that preceded the world). Through <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, one passes over everything. This is also the <span class="key-term">ma'abarta</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — through <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> (= renewal of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> merited through <span class="key-term">emunah</span>), one passes over everything. When one is strong in <span class="key-term">emunah</span> for many years, serving Hashem through <span class="key-term">emunah</span> alone, one merits afterward to know and perceive Hashem with a wondrous knowledge that comes through <span class="key-term">emunah</span> — in the aspect of <span class="zohar-quote">matei v'lo matei</span> <span class="bracket-def">[reaching and not reaching]</span>. This is "<span class="transliteration">Lachazos b'no'am Hashem ul'vaker b'Heichalo</span>" — "To gaze at the pleasantness of Hashem and to visit His Sanctuary" <span class="source">(Tehillim 27:4)</span> — which Dovid HaMelech requested all his days.</p>

<p>Therefore our essential hope now — to leave this bitter <span class="key-term">galus</span>, to nullify the <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span> of the <span class="key-term">Geulah</span> (intensifying now collectively and individually for each person seeking the holy path) — rests on the power of the Tzaddikim, the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span> (very great <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>). Therefore now one must be especially careful to wear <span class="key-term">Tefillin of Rabbeinu Tam</span> — to hasten the <span class="key-term">Geulah</span> collectively and individually, speedily in our days. Each person must merit the redemption of his soul from this bitter <span class="key-term">galus</span> of <span class="key-term">havlei olam hazeh</span>. One must endure actual <span class="key-term">chevlei leidah</span> and <span class="key-term">kishui holadah</span>, especially in the beginning — crying many cries and groans of "<span class="transliteration">Ach!</span>" and "<span class="transliteration">Avoi!</span>" and many turns and twists — before meriting to give birth to the holiness of one's <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> and redeem it from <span class="key-term">galus</span>. May it be fulfilled speedily: "<span class="transliteration">Rani akarah lo yaladah</span>" <span class="source">(Yeshayah 54:1)</span>; "<span class="transliteration">Ha'ani ashbir v'lo olid... ki chalah gam yaldah Tziyon es baneha</span>" <span class="source">(Yeshayah 66:9)</span> — speedily in our days, Amen.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 33 =============== -->
<h3>§ 33</h3>

<p>This is why "<span class="transliteration">Uva l'Tziyon Go'el</span>" is juxtaposed to <span class="bold-concept">Mizmor "<span class="transliteration">Ya'ancha</span>"</span>. It is explained in the <span class="key-term">Kavanos</span> that Mizmor <span class="transliteration">Ya'ancha</span> is recited to give birth to the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">shefa</span> and draw them into this world. Therefore there are <span class="bold-concept">seventy words</span> in Mizmor <span class="transliteration">Ya'ancha</span> — corresponding to the <span class="bold-concept">seventy cries</span> the woman in labor must cry before giving birth.</p>

<p>The <span class="key-term">Geulah</span> = birth, which comes after <span class="key-term">chevlei leidah</span> = <span class="key-term">Chevlei Mashiach</span>. The <span class="key-term">chevlei leidah</span> are seventy cries — for the essential birth is the birth of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>: to renew one's <span class="key-term">avodah</span> and awaken from sleep (= <span class="key-term">ibur</span>). Therefore they are seventy cries — corresponding to the <span class="bold-concept">seventy faces of Torah</span> = the totality of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> that must be birthed and drawn, to awaken from one's sleep and fall, to regain all the seventy faces one has lost, and to begin living anew. This is the essential <span class="key-term">Geulah</span>, individually and collectively. "<span class="transliteration">Uva l'Tziyon Go'el ul'shavei fesha b'Yaakov</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">ul'shavei fesha</span>" precisely = the <span class="key-term">ba'alei teshuvah</span> who renew the days that passed in darkness = renewal of <span class="key-term">chiyus</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Hashiveinu Hashem eilecha v'nashuvah, chadeish yameinu k'kedem.</span>" After Mizmor <span class="transliteration">Ya'ancha</span> (= <span class="key-term">chevlei leidah</span>), we say "<span class="transliteration">Uva l'Tziyon Go'el</span>" — speedily in our days, Amen.</p>


